{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition, how does Suade Labs address both domain knowledge requirements and user preferences in its AI model tuning?", "answer": "Suade Labs uses a human-in-the-loop approach due to domain knowledge needs and regulatory risk, and a human-over-the-loop approach for model tuning to accommodate user preferences on false positives/negatives.", "evidence": [{"page": 33, "quote": "As Suade's solution requires a certain degree of domain knowledge from human experts, and given that the cost of regulatory non-compliance as a result of incorrect recommendations made by the AI solution will be significant to users, Suade has thus adopted a human-in-the-loop approach for its AI solution."}, {"page": 33, "quote": "Suade adopts a human-over-the-loop approach so that the AI model can be tuned to account for the differing preferences of its users with respect to whether the algorithm produces results that favours false positives or false negatives."}]},

  {"question": "How does the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition recommend organisations ensure both data quality and minimise inherent bias in AI model development?", "answer": "Organisations should address data quality factors like accuracy, completeness, and veracity, and mitigate bias by using heterogeneous and complete datasets, avoiding premature removal of data attributes.", "evidence": [{"page": 38, "quote": "Ensuring data quality: Organisations are encouraged to understand and address factors that may affect the quality of data, such as: ... The accuracy of the dataset, ... completeness ... veracity ... relevance ... integrity ... usability ... Human interventions ..."}, {"page": 39, "quote": "organisations can mitigate the risk of inherent bias by having a heterogeneous dataset (i.e. collecting data from a variety of reliable sources). Another way is to ensure the dataset is as complete as possible, both from the perspective of data attributes and data items. Premature removal of data attributes can make it difficult to identify and address inherent bias."}]},

  {"question": "In the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition, what is the difference between repeatability and reproducibility in AI model evaluation?", "answer": "Repeatability is about consistent results within an organisation; reproducibility is about independent teams achieving the same results using the same method and documentation.", "evidence": [{"page": 50, "quote": "While repeatability refers to the internal repetition of results within one's organisation, reproducibility refers to the ability of an independent verification team to produce the same results using the same AI method based on the documentation made by the organisation."}]},

  {"question": "According to the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition, what does 'data lineage' mean and what are its three types?", "answer": "Data lineage means tracing data from source to end-use and vice versa. The three types are backward, forward, and end-to-end data lineage.", "evidence": [{"page": 37, "quote": "Understanding the lineage of data: This means knowing where the data originally came from, how it was collected, curated and moved within the organisation, and how its accuracy is maintained over time. ... There are three types of data lineage: a. i. Backward data lineage looks at the data from its end-use and backdating it to its source. ii. Forward data lineage begins at the data's source and follows it through to its end-use. iii. End-to-end data lineage combines the two and looks at the entire solution from both the data's source to its end-use and from its end-use to its source."}]},

  {"question": "For the company Grab, what two main reasons led to adopting a human-out-of-the-loop approach for trip allocation AI, as described in the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition?", "answer": "Grab adopted a human-out-of-the-loop approach because of the high volume of real-time trip allocations and the low severity of harm from suboptimal allocations.", "evidence": [{"page": 34, "quote": "Among other factors, Grab considered that: (1) it is not technically feasible for a human to make such high volume of trip allocations in a short amount of time; and (2) there is often little or no harm to life should there be less than optimal trip allocations. Hence, Grab decided to adopt a human-out-of-the-loop approach for its AI model deployed for trip allocation, while continuously reviewing the AI model to ensure optimal performance."}]},

  {"question": "In the Singapore Model AI Governance Framework 2nd Edition, which US legal standard does pymetrics use to ensure fairness in its AI hiring models, and what does this standard require?", "answer": "Pymetrics uses the 'four-fifths rule' from the US EEOC, requiring the selection rate for any protected group to be at least 80% of the majority group's rate.", "evidence": [{"page": 42, "quote": "As a prehire assessment, pymetrics models must pass a test known as 'the fourfifths rule', which is commonly cited in employment law. According to US' Equal Employment Opportunity Commission ('EEOC'), the selection rate for any legally protected group must be at least 80% of the selection rate for the majority group."}]}
]}
